Confirmed working end-to-end. Here's the full picture:

## Root cause

Zod **3.22.0** replaced the old, comprehensive email regex with a much stricter, simpler one (PR #2157, "Make email regex reasonable"):

| Version | Local-part characters allowed |
|---|---|
| ≤ 3.21.4 | Full RFC 5322 atext set: `! # $ % & ' * + - / = ? ^ _ \` { \| } ~` … |
| 3.22.0+ | Only `A-Z0-9 _ + -` (3.25.x later added `.` and `'`) |

The 3.22.0 regex is:
```js
/^([A-Z0-9_+-]+\.?)*[A-Z0-9_+-]@([A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9\-]*\.)+[A-Z]{2,}$/i
```
so `!` (and `# $ % & * = ? ^ \` { | } ~`) are all rejected now. I verified `a!test@googlecalender.com` passes on 3.21.4 and fails on 3.22.0 through the latest 3.25.76 — and on **zod v4** as well.

## Upstream status

This is exactly known issue [colinhacks/zod#2963](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/2963). The fix PR (#3427) was **never merged**; the issue was closed in April 2024 pointing back at #2157 — the maintainers consider the stricter regex intentional ("immediate usability win for the 98% of users who don't realize `.email()` currently lets through a bunch of garbage"). So don't wait for an upstream fix; use a workaround.

## Workarounds

**1. Replace `.email()` with `.regex()` (recommended).** I verified the HTML spec's `<input type="email">` pattern (what browsers accept) against 15 valid/invalid cases — it accepts `a!test@googlecalender.com`, `test.and!#$%&*+-/=?^_`{|}~.stuff@example.com`, `O'reilly@…`, `user+tag@…`, hyphenated subdomains, and still rejects `@example.com`, `user@`, `user@example..com`, `user@-example.com`, etc.:

```js
import { z } from "zod";

export const email = z.string().regex(
  /^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*\.[a-z]{2,}$/i,
  "Invalid email",
);
```
(Fails with `code: "invalid_string", validation: "regex"` — same shape as `.email()`'s error.) The trailing `\.[a-z]{2,}$` keeps zod's TLD requirement; drop it if you want to match browsers exactly (which allow `user@localhost`). Note: like the browser regex, it tolerates `user..name@…` (consecutive dots).

**2. Keep the `.email()` API everywhere** by monkey-patching (approach from the issue thread):

```js
import { z, type ZodString } from "zod";

const emailRegex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*\.[a-z]{2,}$/i;

z.ZodString.prototype.email = function (message?: string | { message?: string }): ZodString {
  return this._addCheck({
    kind: "regex",
    regex: emailRegex,
    message: typeof message === "string" ? message : message?.message,
  });
};
```

**3. Delegate to a dedicated library** if you need RFC-accurate validation: `z.string().superRefine((v, ctx) => { if (!isEmail(v)) ctx.addIssue({ code: z.ZodIssueCode.custom, message: "Invalid email" }); })` with `validator` or `email-validator`.

Downgrading to 3.21.4 technically "fixes" it but is the worst option — it's old and the regex was replaced for performance reasons (the old one was very slow and could be ReDoS-attacked).

