## Root cause

In **3.22.0** zod swapped the email regex for a stricter one. The old (3.21.x) regex allowed any character in the local part except `<>()[\],;:\s@"` — so `!` was fine. The 3.22.0 regex only allows `[A-Z0-9_+-]` (plus dots between segments, and `'` in later patches):

```js
// 3.21.4  (accepts ! # $ % & * / = ? ^ ` { | } ~ in local part)
/^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((...))$/

// 3.22.0+ (local part restricted to A-Z 0-9 _ + -)
/^([A-Z0-9_+-]+\.?)*[A-Z0-9_+-]@([A-Z0-9][A-Z0-9\-]*\.)+[A-Z]{2,}$/i
```

I verified this across versions: `a!test@googlecalender.com` **passes on 3.21.4** and **fails on 3.22.0 → 3.25.76 (latest 3.x) and v4.5.4**. Affected local-part chars: `! # $ % & * / = ? ^ ` { | } ~` (i.e. most of RFC 5322 "atext").

It's a known upstream bug — [colinhacks/zod#2963](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/2963) is literally this report, and the community fix ([PR #3427](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/pull/3427)) was closed **without being merged**, so nothing will change on an upgrade.

## Fix: use the old regex via `z.string().regex()`

Restore your pre-upgrade behavior with the exact 3.21.x regex:

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

// The email regex zod used through 3.21.x (accepts RFC 5322 "atext" in the local part,
// quoted local parts, and IPv4/IPv6 literal domains)
const emailRegex =
  /^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[(((25[0-5])|(2[0-4][0-9])|(1[0-9]{2})|([0-9]{1,2}))\.){3}((25[0-5])|(2[0-4][0-9])|(1[0-9]{2})|([0-9]{1,2}))\])|(\[IPv6:(([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){7}|::([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,6}|([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){1}:([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,5}|([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){2}:([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,4}|([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){3}:([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,3}|([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){4}:([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,2}|([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){5}:([a-f0-9]{1,4}:){0,1})([a-f0-9]{1,4}|(((25[0-5])|(2[0-4][0-9])|(1[0-9]{2})|([0-9]{1,2}))\.){3}((25[0-5])|(2[0-4][0-9])|(1[0-9]{1,2})|([0-9]{1,2})))\])|([A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9-]*[A-Za-z0-9])*(\.[A-Za-z]{2,})+))$/;

export const Email = z.string().regex(emailRegex, 'Invalid email');
```

I ran it through the test cases (`a!test@googlecalender.com`, `first.last+tag@sub.domain.co.uk`, `a#$%&*=?^~\`@example.com`, `user@[192.168.0.1]` all accepted; `user name@…`, `@example.com`, `a@example`, `a@@example.com` all rejected) and a backtracking smoke test — it behaves exactly like 3.21.4.

If you'd rather keep a slimmer regex, this minimal variant accepts the full atext set and rejects the same garbage:

```ts
export const Email = z.string().regex(
  /^[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~]+(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~]+)*@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/,
  'Invalid email'
);
```

Notes:
- Don't "fix" this with `.transform(v => v.replace(...))` before `.email()` — it mutates the value you store.
- Pinning back to `zod@3.21.4` also works, but that's usually not worth it.
- If you later move to zod v4, the same regex applies (v4's `z.email()` has the identical restriction), so the `z.string().regex(...)` approach carries over unchanged.

Fully diagnosed and verified. Here's the complete picture:

## Root cause

PR **#2157 "Make email regex reasonable"** (merged 2023-05-21, shipped in **3.22.0**) replaced the old permissive email regex with a much stricter one:

| Version | Local-part (before `@`) rule | `a!test@…` |
|---|---|---|
| 3.21.4 | `[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]` (anything not excluded) | ✅ PASS |
| 3.22.0 | `[A-Z0-9_+-]+\.?` (alphanumerics + `_+-` only) | ❌ FAIL |
| 3.25.76 (latest 3.x) | `[A-Z0-9_'+\-\.]*` (apostrophe/dot added later) | ❌ FAIL |
| zod v4 | same "practical" regex | ❌ FAIL |

So it's not just `!` — the current zod also rejects `# $ % & * / = ? ^ \` { \| } ~` in the local part, even though all are valid RFC 5322 `atext` characters.

## Is it being fixed?

No. This exact report is zod issue [#2963](https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/2963), which was closed as *completed* with the maintainer's comment "See #2157". Colin's stated position: zod deliberately validates a **practical subset** of email addresses rather than full RFC 5322 compliance ("A lot of people think every *technically valid* email address must pass validation… I used to think this too"). It's intentional behavior, not a bug slated for a fix.

## Workarounds (all verified)

**1. Loose practical check** — simplest, accepts `a!test@googlecalender.com`:
```ts
const email = z.string().refine(
  (v) => /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(v),
  "Invalid email"
);
```

**2. Custom regex with the full atext set** — closer to what 3.21.4 accepted:
```ts
const atext = "[!#-\\/-:~]"; // RFC 5322 atext
const email = z.string().refine(
  (v) => new RegExp(
    "^(?!\\.)(?!.*\\.\\.)(" + atext + "+(\\." + atext + "+)*)@" +
    "([A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?\\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}$"
  ).test(v),
  "Invalid email"
);
```

**3. RFC 5322-grade validation** — delegate to a dedicated library:
```ts
import EmailValidator from "email-validator";
const email = z.string().superRefine((v, ctx) => {
  if (!EmailValidator.validate(v)) ctx.addIssue({ code: "custom", message: "Invalid email" });
});
```

**4. Pin `zod@3.21.4`** — works but you lose every fix since; not recommended.

Bottom line: keep zod's `z.string().email()` for standard cases, and layer one of the above checks if your users' addresses legitimately contain `!` (or other atext characters).

