`!` is a valid "atext" character per RFC 5322, so `a!test@googlecalender.com` is a perfectly valid address — but it's no longer in the allowed set. The same regression dropped `# $ % & * = ? ^ ` { | } ~` and quoted local-parts.

**Still not fixed:** I verified it fails on the latest `3.25.76` (they only re-added `'` since) **and** on `zod@4.5.4`.

## Workarounds

**Option 1 — custom regex via `refine`** (recommended; tested against the old 3.21.4 behavior):

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const emailRegex =
  /^[a-zA-Z0-9.!#$%&'*+\/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?(?:\.[a-zA-Z0-9](?:[a-zA-Z0-9-]{0,61}[a-zA-Z0-9])?)*\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$/;

export const email = z.string().refine((val) => emailRegex.test(val), {
  message: "Invalid email",
});

email.parse("a!test@googlecalender.com"); // ✅ passes
email.parse("john.doe+tag@sub.example.co.uk"); // ✅
email.parse("a b@c.com"); // ❌ rejected
```

**Option 2 — restore 3.21.x behavior exactly** (accepts IP-literal domains and quoted local-parts too):

```ts
export const email = z.string().refine(
  (val) =>
    /^(([^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+(\.[^<>()[\]\\.,;:\s@"]+)*)|(".+"))@((\[[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\])|(([A-Za-z0-9\-]+\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}))$/.test(val),
  { message: "Invalid email" }
);
```

**Option 3 — pin `zod@3.21.4`** (not recommended; you'd miss all fixes since).

A reusable `zod/v3/email`-style drop-in is Option 1: it accepts all RFC 5322 atext local-part characters while keeping zod's historical "TLD ≥ 2 letters" strictness.

